title: HLMM Query Status
agents: hlmm
catalog: os/services
license: GPLv3
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check reports whether the HLMON special agent's own query against
 the HLMON REST API succeeded, on the "collector" host the special
 agent rule is assigned to (not on the piggybacked target hosts).

 It exists because a broken or partial HLMON query would otherwise only
 be visible indirectly -- e.g. as stale {HLMM Service Status} checks on
 various hosts -- which is easy to miss. This check makes it visible in
 one place.

 The check goes {CRIT} if the agent could not resolve any hosts or
 services at all (e.g. the HLMON server was unreachable, or
 authentication failed). It goes {WARN} if some, but not all, requests
 failed (e.g. one batch of a paginated /services query returned an
 error while others succeeded) -- the summary shows how many errors
 occurred, and the details list each one with the stage it occurred in
 (hosts, services, or pattern) and the underlying error. Otherwise it is
 {OK} and shows how many hosts and services were matched, and how long
 the query took.

 If the special agent produced no status data at all (e.g. it crashed
 before completing), the check goes {UNKNOWN}.

 A piggybacked target host does not necessarily get a {HLMM Service
 Status} check: if "Import HLMON host status" is enabled but none of the
 host's services matched the configured service name patterns, only the
 {HLMM Host Status} check is created for that host.

 Performance data ({hlmm_status_hosts_matched}, {hlmm_status_services_matched},
 {hlmm_status_duration}) is attached whenever the special agent produced
 any status data, even in the {CRIT}/{WARN} cases -- so a graph of hosts
 or services matched over time can make e.g. a gradually failing HLMON
 query visible before it becomes a hard failure.

discovery:
 One service is created once the special agent has produced any status
 data.
